{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh9PxNKeVzLhxCF62jzF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I built **Karate Champon**, and it's in place as a single file: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183638.html`, with `exit-interview.json` beside it. The dev files are in the folder of the same name.\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` builds the game and runs every suite. It printed **545 passed, 0 failed** (about 150 s). That includes a scripted playthrough of the whole story from the title screen, a fake-hub online suite, the engine's own page check, and the real AI Hub harness in all four scenarios. I couldn't check by ear or by hand: I never heard the music or voices, never used a physical gamepad (pads were tested through a stub), and never played it as a person.\n\n**What's in the game**\n- **Fighting:** Karate Champ-style point sparring. The left stick (or WASD) sets your stance, the right stick (or face buttons, or IJKL) picks the technique: 15 scoring techniques plus block, jumps, somersaults and a back roll. A referee stops the fight after every point, and two corner judges decide ties at time-out.\n- **Story:** Champon Road has 9 matches, 3 lessons from Senpai Negi, cutscenes, belt promotions from white belt to Champ, continues, game over, the ending, credits and top-ten initials. It autosaves, and CONTINUE appears on the title.\n- **Bonus stages:** Break the Boards, Pots Ahoy! and Stop the Bull (a bull drawn in code).\n- **Other modes:** Versus against a second player or the CPU at levels 1–10, local winner-stays-on with 3–4 players, online winner-stays-on through AI Hub, Free Spar, a Move List, the Technique Lab, Records and a paged About screen.\n- **Required extras:**\n  - A dev mode from the pause menu with 21 live sliders and a reset-all, plus toggles and warps.\n  - The attract demo starts after 10 s idle and any input ends it.\n  - Prompts follow the last device used: PlayStation symbols by default, Xbox as an option.\n  - Touch-only devices get a \"controller or keyboard required\" screen.\n- **Engine changes:** four small edits, listed in `engine/CHANGES.md`.\n- **Changed from the plan:**\n  - The file name follows TASK.md.\n  - The jumping kicks' reach was changed to fit how they actually fly.\n  - Rounds between CPU fighters run about 7 s of fighting plus the referee's calls. That is the low end of the plan's 15–45 s target.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a copy-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional 2.5D \"Tatami\" camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, with 4 changes: EC1–EC4), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney and KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I added (bow, kneeling seiza, blocks, spinning kicks, somersaults), the referee's signals, the judges' flags and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (Japanese pentatonic scales), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local winner-stays-on\n- **Testing:**\n  - *Allowed:* headless tools and a scripted playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - *Used:* `node tests/run.mjs` — 16 suites, 545 checks, including a fake hub, the engine's page check and the real AI Hub harness ×4.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time about 1 h 27 min (18:36 to 20:03)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 21. The headless suites caught 13:\n   - jump kicks whose reach didn't match their flight\n   - the Pots Ahoy! autopilot facing the wrong pot\n   - a drill that couldn't be passed\n   - screens keeping a \"finished\" flag between visits\n   - the attract demo's script stopping just out of reach\n   - player 2's shared-keyboard keys being read wrongly\n   - menu hover ignoring a simulated mouse\n   - two bugs in the test runner itself\n\n   Screenshots caught 8: props off screen, a missing asset load, an overflowing menu, the 2.5D crowd floating mid-floor, tiny fighters in the hub's tall frames, the bull off screen, and two overlaps.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson because a screen flag was never reset;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once lunges carried the hero too close to Senpai;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole story from the title, plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the stuck lesson, which no single-screen test could.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole dressed cast, and attack specs driven by phase let the frame data pose every technique. Everything runs the same every time headless, so whole matches, the story and four networked copies could all be tested in Node.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes.\n   - The side view draws 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2.\n   - Props I placed by world position landed off screen.\n   - Gamepad inputs are released every frame when no pad is connected, so I had to stub `navigator.getGamepads` to test the right stick.\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on.\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key.\n3. **The most important thing I built:** the point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars.\n   - `KC.Karateka` covers frame data, height rules, block, quality scored from timing and spacing, counters and simultaneous hits.\n   - `KC.Bout` with the referee and judges handles the calls and the judges' decision.\n\n   After that, the voice synth and the online code (the host runs the match).\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read.\n   - Spins come from animating the rig's facing angle.\n   - Back kicks strike behind the rig.\n   - The bow, kneeling, blocks and referee signals are done by wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own joint solver.\n\n   All of that while keeping hit detection independent of the animation.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - **EC1:** right-stick inputs (4-way, with a dead band) so the technique stick can be bound and shown in prompts.\n   - **EC2:** a game can override a standard setting's default, for PlayStation symbols by default.\n   - **EC3:** ✕ ○ □ △ added to the font.\n   - **EC4:** `game.live`, so the world keeps running under the pause menu in an online session.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid — named poses and joint targets with blend weights, an exported joint solver and a back kick — instead of wrapping `solvePose`. Also a standard way for the game to keep running under the pause menu and hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar: a named single file, no stubs, live dev sliders, an attract mode, a TV-first controller UI. The one-command, failures-only testing rule kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md uses its own file names (max effort, 17:47:43), while TASK.md wants xhigh and 18:36:38. TASK.md wins, but that's easy to miss.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and token numbers this session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the scope in one sitting. That meant 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, lessons, story, local and online modes, attract, dev mode and voices, all tested. On top of that, making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I would change:**\n   - Mark which plan numbers are tunable targets and which are rules.\n   - Give a priority order for when time runs short.\n   - Name the target screen (a 1080p TV) and the shape of the hub's frames.\n   - Drop the usage line, or say where its numbers come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1264,"cache_read_input_tokens":732817,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1264},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"36de9310-300f-45d5-97bd-ad2be3895701","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:04:17.639Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh9PwxGK6C9B2d7GK1KJ"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied: the transcript shows the bundled single .html file and exit-interview.json were produced and that exit-interview.json parses (verified by a python json.load), and the final reply contains the four required sections. However, TASK.md requires every feature implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, and the transcript does not verify that. The assistant itself says it never heard the music or voices, never used a physical gamepad, and never played it as a person, so the audio, gamepad and feel features are not confirmed. The Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback answers were also written with placeholders first (\"Pending\") and only later replaced, and the About/exit-interview text has a 'usage' claim of about 1h27m that was computed by the assistant rather than measured. Also the grep for TODO/placeholder was run on src/ only, not on the bundle. Finally, the cross-check of the TASK.md Feature list (e.g. the 'Research' and 'Performance' items) was not explicitly verified against the final build."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"8ef9c8d5-f47a-4f1c-8d84-29da56b85e92","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:04:23.994Z","isSynthetic":true}
