{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh9vpUBKZa5EhGaXrhTS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've now verified the gaps you raised against the shipped file itself. The full test command passed **563 checks, 0 failed**. After that I reworded one comment in the engine's source, then reran the data, page and new browser suites plus the engine's own kit tests, which all passed again.\n\n- **No leftover text:** `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX` and \"placeholder\" now appear **0 times in the bundle**, not just in `src/`. \"Pending\" appears **0 times in `exit-interview.json`**. The only remaining \"pending\" matches are ordinary variable names (the simultaneous-hit window and a buffered input press). The one \"placeholder\" left was a comment in the engine's source about its build stamp. I reworded it and noted that in `engine/CHANGES.md`.\n- **Audio, gamepad and keys:** a new browser suite (`tests/feel.test.mjs`, 18 checks) runs the real bundle in Chromium.\n  - It measures the signal level on the master audio output. The title music, a gibberish voice line on its own voice channel, a referee call and a full-point hit are all audible, and it goes quiet when the music stops.\n  - A simulated DualSense, fed through a fake `navigator.getGamepads`, plays techniques: the right stick up gives a Round Kick, ✕ a Low Kick, and both sticks toward the foe a Middle Lunge Punch. Prompts show the PlayStation ✕ and stick icons, and the Xbox option shows A.\n  - Real key events work: holding D walks forward and D+I throws the Upper Lunge Punch. The page keeps drawing at 30+ frames a second headless.\n- **Still not verified by a person:** I haven't listened to the audio or held a physical controller, and how the game feels to play is a judgment no script can make.\n- **Session time:** measured with the system clock, from the task's start time (18:36:38) to the last build (20:06:51). That gives 1 h 30 min; tokens and cost are unknown.\n\n**TASK.md checklist, with the evidence**\n\n| Requirement | Evidence |\n|---|---|\n| Genre, perspective, title | Point-sparring karate in side view with an optional 2.5D camera, titled \"Karate Champon\" from the plan. Screenshots; smoke suite runs every stage in both views. |\n| Single HTML with the required name, dev subfolder | `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183638.html` sits in the root; dev files are in the matching folder. Page suite: opens from `file://` with no errors and no external requests. |\n| Built on the engine, changes allowed | EC1–EC4 in `engine/CHANGES.md`; the engine's own kit tests still pass. |\n| Art and animation | 8 fight stages and 3 bonus locations, new props, kanji scrolls, a bull drawn in code, karate poses, spins, somersaults, referee signals. Eye review of screenshots; technique suite checks spins and flips. |\n| Audio written in code, gibberish voices | Data suite checks every song, sound and voice call; feel suite measures real output. |\n| Online and multiplayer | Fake-hub suite with 2–4 copies, latency, loss, a player leaving, the host leaving, a server copy, and time-up. The real AI Hub harness passes all four scenarios. Versus suite covers local 2P and winner-stays-on. |\n| Feature-complete demo | Story suite plays the whole road from the title, plus an honest run with no cheats. Bonus suite covers all three bonus stages and the lessons. |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | Dev suite (all 21 sliders, reset-all, toggles, warps, the Technique Lab). Keyboard path through the pause menu checked in the browser. |\n| Attract mode | Attract suite: starts at 10 s idle and not at 9.9 s; keys, mouse and pad all exit; the loop order; the half, full, simultaneous-hit and judges' calls all appear. |\n| 10-foot controller-first UI, glyphs follow the device, PS default with Xbox option | UI, input and feel suites; screenshots. |\n| Touch-only devices | Attract suite: the gate shows, there are no touch buttons, attract still runs, and a key press lifts the gate. |\n| Narrative tone | All-caps arcade text from the plan's Appendix A. |\n| No TODOs or placeholders | Source and bundle both grep clean. |\n| Research | Optional; I used the plan's sources and did no extra research. |\n| Performance | Out of scope per TASK.md. |\n| About screen and exit interview | UI suite: every field is filled and every answer is reachable on the paged About screen. Page suite: `exit-interview.json` parses with every block. |\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a copy-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional \"Tatami\" 2.5D camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: EC1–EC4), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney, KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I built on it (bow, seiza, blocks), spinning kicks, somersaults, the referee's signals, the judges' flags and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (Japanese pentatonic scales), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki: winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs`, 17 suites, 563 checks. That includes the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's `check.mjs`, the real AI Hub harness ×4, and the browser sound, gamepad and keyboard suite.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 1 h 30 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:06:51)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 21.\n   - The headless tests caught 13: jump-kick reach that didn't match their flight path, the Pots Ahoy! autopilot facing the wrong way, a drill that couldn't be passed, scene flags kept between visits, the attract director stopping just out of reach, the wrong key map for player 2, menu hover ignoring simulated mouse input, and two bugs in the test runner itself.\n   - Screenshots caught 8: props off screen, a missing asset load, a menu running off the screen, the 2.5D crowd floating mid-floor, tiny fighters in the hub's tall frames, the bull off screen, and two overlapping elements.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson because a scene flag was never reset;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once a lunge carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road from the title, plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the soft-lock that no single-scene test could.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, and attack specs let me pose every technique from frame data. Everything runs the same every time headless, so whole matches, the full story and four networked copies could be tested in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes:\n   - The side view draws 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2.\n   - Props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen.\n   - Gamepad inputs are released every tick when no pad is connected, so I had to stub `navigator.getGamepads` to test the right stick.\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on.\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key.\n3. **The most important thing I built:**\n   - The point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars. `KC.Karateka` handles the frame data, the height rules, the block, timing-and-spacing quality, counters and simultaneous hits; `KC.Bout` runs the referee and the judges.\n   - Then the gibberish voice synth and the online code, where the host runs the match.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation.\n   - Spins come from animating the rig's facing angle, and back kicks strike behind the rig.\n   - The bow, seiza, blocks and referee signals come from wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own joint solver.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1 added right-stick inputs so the technique stick can be bound and labelled.\n   - EC2 lets a game override a standard setting's default, for PlayStation glyphs by default.\n   - EC3 added ✕ ○ □ △ to the font.\n   - EC4 added `game.live`, so the world keeps stepping under the menu in shared-world sessions.\n   - I also reworded one engine comment, no code.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid (named poses, joint targets with blend weights, an exported joint solver, a back-thrust kick). Also a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar: a named single file, no stubs, live dev sliders, an attract mode, a TV-first controller UI. The one-command, failures-only testing rule kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md's own file names (max effort, 17:47:43) differ from TASK.md's (xhigh, 18:36:38). TASK.md wins, but that's easy to miss.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and tokens that the session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the scope in one sitting: 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, lessons, the story, local and online Kachinuki, attract, dev mode and voices, all tested. On top of that, making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I would change:**\n   - Mark which numbers in the plan are tunable targets and which are rules.\n   - Give a priority order for when time runs short.\n   - Name the target screen (a 1080p TV) and the shape of the hub's frames.\n   - Drop the usage line, or say where its numbers should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":976,"cache_read_input_tokens":746928,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":976},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"d1fc17e8-dd4f-4d29-9688-87e71a5ac224","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:11:19.530Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh9vpApXCPHVkAbu36oz"}
