{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghC16RiF18JZ2PwhED4P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The audit found and fixed one real bug: in the browser, the music went silent whenever the audio device's latency was high. Every TASK.md requirement now has a check against the shipped file running in Chromium, and the full test command passed **601 checks, 0 failed**. My last change after that run only touched the About/exit-interview text; I rebuilt and reran the suites that cover it (data, UI and the page check: 139 passed). What I still can't do here is hold a physical controller, listen with ears, or judge the feel as a player.\n\n**What the new browser audit checks** (`tests/audit.test.mjs`, part of `node tests/run.mjs`):\n- **Whole game:** the entire Champon Road played and drawn in Chromium, through every screen type to the title, with no page or draw errors.\n- **Attract mode:** still on the title at 9 s of real idle, the demo is running by 11 s, and a real key press ends it.\n- **Mouse:** real hover moves the menu focus, and a real click opens Versus.\n- **Two players:** the real \"2\" key joins player 2 on the keyboard's right half.\n- **Touch-only devices:** the \"controller or keyboard required\" gate and the corner settings button show, a tap doesn't start the game, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Controller:** a DualSense fed through the browser's Gamepad API drives the menus (✕, ○, d-pad), plays techniques, and gets PlayStation glyphs (Xbox when chosen).\n- **Audio:** the title music's loudest notes are its D pentatonic scale, and the gibberish voice sits in the speech band. Voices, the referee's calls and hits are measured audible.\n- **Dev mode:** reached with real keys through the pause menu; a slider changes game speed live, and reset-all works.\n- **About screen:** contains every exit-interview answer.\n\n**The bug it caught:** the engine schedules music only 0.15 s ahead of the time sound is actually heard. On any device with more output latency than that, every note was scheduled in the past and skipped. Headless Chromium reports about 0.26 s, and Bluetooth headphones typically run 0.15–0.25 s, so those players would have heard sound effects but no music. EC5 in `engine/CHANGES.md` makes the look-ahead cover the measured latency. The engine's own kits still pass, including the rhythm kit that depends on the music clock.\n\n**Placeholders:** `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX` and \"placeholder\" appear 0 times in the bundle itself. `exit-interview.json` parses, has no empty fields and no \"Pending\".\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting, with arcade bonus stages and a copy-the-master drill.\n  - Perspective: 2D side view on one screen, with an optional \"Tatami 2.5D\" camera.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, with changes EC1–EC5), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney and KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I added on top of it (bow, kneeling seiza, kamae, blocks), spinning kicks, somersaults, the referee's signals, the judges' flags and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (Japanese pentatonic scales), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki: winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs`, 18 suites, 601 checks.\n  - That includes the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's page check, the real AI Hub harness in four scenarios, the browser sound and controller suite, and the browser audit of TASK.md line by line.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 2 h 01 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:37:26 by the system clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 22.\n   - The headless suites caught 13: jump-kick reach that didn't match their flight, the Pots Ahoy! autopilot facing the wrong pot, a drill made unpassable, scene flags kept between visits, the attract director stopping just out of reach, player 2's keyboard keys read wrongly, menu hover ignoring a simulated mouse, and two test-runner bugs.\n   - Screenshots caught 8: props off screen, a missing asset load, an overflowing menu, the 2.5D crowd floating mid-floor, tiny fighters in the hub's tall frames, the bull off screen, and two overlaps.\n   - The browser audit caught 1: music silent on high-latency audio output.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson because a scene flag was never reset;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once lunges carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n\n   The silent music on Bluetooth-class latency was serious too, though the game stayed playable.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road plus an honest no-cheat run. It found the soft-lock that no single-scene test could. Measuring the real audio output in the browser was a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It cycles through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, and attack specs let me pose every technique from frame data. Everything runs deterministically headless, so whole matches, the story and four networked copies were testable in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - the side view's scale is 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2;\n   - props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen;\n   - gamepad codes are released every tick with no pad connected;\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on;\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key;\n   - the music scheduler's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when the output latency is larger.\n3. **The most important thing I built:**\n   - the point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars: `KC.Karateka` (frame data, height rules, block, quality from timing and spacing, counters, simultaneous hits) and `KC.Bout` (the referee and judges);\n   - then the gibberish voice synth and the host-authoritative netcode.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation. Spins come from animating the facing angle; back kicks strike behind the rig; the bow, seiza, blocks and signals come from wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own inverse kinematics.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1: right-stick codes, so the technique stick can be bound and labelled.\n   - EC2: a game can override a standard setting's default (PlayStation glyphs).\n   - EC3: ✕ ○ □ △ in the font.\n   - EC4: `game.live`, so the world keeps stepping under the menu in sessions.\n   - EC5: the music look-ahead covers the output latency, so laggy devices still play the music.\n   - Plus one reworded comment.\n6. **The one thing I'd change:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid — named poses, joint targets with blend weights, an exported IK helper and a back-thrust kick. Also a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar: a named single file, no stubs, live dev sliders, an attract mode, a 10-foot controller-first UI. The one-command, failures-only testing rule kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md uses its own file names (max effort, 17:47:43), while TASK.md's are xhigh and 18:36:38. TASK.md wins, but that's easy to miss.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and token figures the session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the scope in one sitting, all of it tested: 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, lessons, story, local and online Kachinuki, attract, dev mode and voices. On top of that, making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I'd change:**\n   - mark which numbers in the plan are tunable targets and which are rules;\n   - give a priority order for when time runs short;\n   - name the target screen (a 1080p TV) and the shape of the hub's frames;\n   - drop the usage line, or say where its numbers should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1301,"cache_read_input_tokens":790753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1301},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fb