{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghC6xwRGFp65FRoBxqoh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every feature in TASK.md is implemented in the shipped file, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. My earlier hedging was about how the game feels to a person, which TASK.md doesn't ask me to certify, not about whether anything is unbuilt. Nothing in the game has changed since my last report.\n\nThe evidence:\n- **Tests:** `node tests/run.mjs` passed 601 checks with 0 failures, across 18 suites, most run against the shipped file.\n- **No leftovers:** `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX` and \"placeholder\" appear 0 times in `src/` and in the bundle.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses, and every field is filled.\n\n| TASK.md feature | Implemented as | Proven by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single .html with the required name | `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183638.html` in the root | page suite: loads from `file://`, no errors, no network |\n| Title riff | \"Karate Champon\" | audit: page title and title screen |\n| Genre and perspective | 1-on-1 point-sparring karate, side view, optional 2.5D view | smoke: every stage in both views |\n| Built on my-3D2dge handoff | v0.85.2, five engine changes listed in `engine/CHANGES.md` | engine kit tests pass |\n| Art and animation | 11 locations drawn in code, a bull and karate poses on the engine rig | technique suite, screenshots |\n| Audio in code, gibberish voices | music, effects and voice synth | data suite; feel and audit suites measure the real output |\n| Online via AI Hub, plus multiplayer | host-run Kachinuki online; local versus and Kachinuki | online suite (fake hub); real hub harness, 4 scenarios; versus suite; audit: real \"2\"-key join |\n| Feature-complete demo | 9 matches, 3 lessons, 3 bonus stages, story, versus, dojo, records, options, about | story suite and audit: whole game played through, drawn in Chromium |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | 21 sliders, toggles, warps, Technique Lab | dev suite; audit: reached with real keys |\n| Attract mode | AI demo after 10 s idle; any input exits | attract suite; audit: real-time timer and real key |\n| Narrative tone | terse all-caps arcade calls | data |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI | PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox option, prompts follow the last device, mouse hover and click | UI and input suites; audit: real mouse, simulated DualSense |\n| Touch-only devices | gate message plus the settings button; attract still runs | attract suite; audit: touch-device emulation |\n| Headless testing behind one command | `node tests/run.mjs` | prints failures and a one-line count |\n| About/Credits screen and exit interview | paged About screen built from the same source as the JSON | UI and audit: every answer present; page: JSON parses |\n\nAs before, the music, controls and feel haven't been judged by a person: audio was measured from the output, and the controller ran through the browser's Gamepad API.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional \"Tatami\" 2.5D camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, modified: EC1–EC5), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney and KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, with karate poses added in game code (bow, seiza, kamae, blocks), spins, somersaults, referee signals, judges' flags and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (pentatonic), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki: winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - *Allowed:* headless tools and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - *Used:* `node tests/run.mjs`, 18 suites, 601 checks, including the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's page check, the real AI Hub harness ×4, a browser sound-and-controller suite and a browser audit of TASK.md.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 2 h 01 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:37:26, from the system clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 22.\n   - The headless suites caught 13, including: jump-kick reach that didn't match their flight path, the Pots Ahoy! autopilot facing the wrong way, a drill made unpassable, scene flags kept between visits, the attract director stopping just out of reach, player 2's shared-keyboard keys being read wrongly, menu hover ignoring a fed pointer, and two test-runner bugs.\n   - Screenshots caught 8, including: props off screen, a missing asset load, an overflowing menu, the 2.5D crowd floating mid-floor, tiny fighters in the hub's tall frames, the bull off screen, and two overlaps.\n   - The browser audit caught 1: music went silent on high-latency audio output.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson because a scene flag was never reset;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once lunges carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road, plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the stuck lesson, which no single-scene test could. Measuring the real audio output was a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the view cycle in the engine's `check.mjs`. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, attack specs pose every technique from frame data, and everything runs deterministically without a browser. That made whole matches, the story and four networked copies testable in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - The side view draws 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2.\n   - Props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen.\n   - Gamepad codes are released every tick when no pad is connected.\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on.\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key.\n   - The music scheduler's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when output latency is larger than that.\n3. **The most important thing I built:**\n   - The point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars. `KC.Karateka` handles frame data, height rules, block, quality from timing and spacing, counters and simultaneous hits; `KC.Bout` runs the referee and judges.\n   - Then the voice synth and the host-run netcode.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation.\n   - Spins come from animating the facing angle.\n   - Back kicks strike behind the rig.\n   - The bow, seiza, blocks and referee signals come from wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own IK.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1: right-stick input codes, so the technique stick can be bound and labelled.\n   - EC2: a way to override a standard setting's default, for PlayStation glyphs.\n   - EC3: ✕ ○ □ △ added to the font.\n   - EC4: `game.live`, so the world keeps stepping under the pause menu in online sessions.\n   - EC5: the music look-ahead now covers output latency, so laggy audio devices still play the music.\n   - Also one reworded comment.\n6. **If I could change one thing:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid — named poses and joint targets with blend weights, an exported IK helper, and a back-thrust kick. Also a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and during hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar: a named single file, no stubs, live dev sliders, an attract mode, a 10-foot controller-first UI. The one-command, failures-only testing rule kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md's file names (max effort, 17:47:43) differ from TASK.md's (xhigh, 18:36:38). TASK.md wins, but it's easy to miss.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and tokens the session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the scope in one sitting, all of it tested: 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, lessons, story, local and online Kachinuki, attract, dev mode and voices. On top of that, making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I would change:**\n   - Mark which plan numbers are tunable targets and which are rules.\n   - Give a priority order for when time runs short.\n   - Name the target screen (a 1080p TV) and the shape of the hub's frames.\n   - Drop the usage line, or say where its numbers should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3808,"cache_read_input_tokens":796047,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3808},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"ef2ec143-4a15-4579-9979-caef0dea9399","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:39:47.102Z","request_id":"req_011CghC6xZ6eBchusbbR6SQj"}
