k at the second lesson, Lesson II becoming unpassable once lunges carried the hero too close, and player 2 on a shared keyboard getting the wrong keys.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the soft-lock that no single-scene test could. Measuring the real audio output was a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the view cycle in the engine's `check.mjs`. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, attack specs pose every technique from frame data, and everything runs deterministically headless. That made matches, the story and four networked copies testable in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - The side view draws 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2.\n   - Props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen.\n   - Gamepad codes are released every tick when no pad is connected.\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on.\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key.\n   - The music scheduler's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when the output latency is larger than that.\n3. **The most important thing I built:** the point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars. `KC.Karateka` handles frame data, height rules, the block, quality from timing and spacing, counters and simultaneous hits; `KC.Bout` runs the referee and the judges. After that, the gibberish voice synth and the host-authoritative netcode.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation. Spins animate the facing angle and back kicks strike behind the rig. The bow, seiza, blocks and referee signals come from wrapping `rig.solvePose` with