{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCBbv2JqvsLmMzQgSag","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm stopping here. What this check asks for now is a person playing the game, real gamepad hardware, or an independent auditor. None of those exist in this environment, and TASK.md rules out using subagents to audit or judge. Another automated pass would not change that, so I haven't run one.\n\nNothing has changed since my last reply:\n- **Deliverables:** the single `.html` and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root, and the JSON parses with every field filled.\n- **Features:** every TASK.md feature is implemented, with no TODO, FIXME or placeholder text in the source or the bundle.\n- **Tests:** `node tests/run.mjs` passed 601 checks with 0 failed.\n\nYour part is to play it with a real controller and listen to it. If it holds up, clear the goal with `/goal clear`. If anything is wrong, tell me what and I'll fix it.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional 2.5D \"Tatami\" camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the full engine, modified with EC1–EC5), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney and KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I added in game code (bow, seiza, kamae, blocks), spins, somersaults, the referee's signals, the judges' flags, and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (pentatonic), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki, winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs` — 18 suites, 601 checks, including the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's page check, the real AI Hub harness ×4, a browser sound and controller suite, and a browser audit of TASK.md.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 2 h 01 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:37:26 by the system clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 22.\n   - The headless suites caught 13, including: jump-kick reach that didn't match the jump's flight, the Pots Ahoy! autopilot facing the wrong way, a drill that could not be passed, scene flags kept between visits, the attract director stopping just short of reach, player 2's keys being read wrongly, menu hover ignoring a fed pointer, and two bugs in the test runner itself.\n   - Screenshots caught 8, including: props off screen, a missing asset load, a menu running off the screen, the 2.5D crowd floating mid-floor, fighters too small in the hub's tall frames, the bull off screen, and two overlapping elements.\n   - The browser audit caught 1: music going silent on high-latency audio output.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson because a scene flag was never reset;\n   - Lesson II became unpassable once lunges carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road, plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the stuck lesson, which no single-scene test could. Measuring the real audio output was a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, attack specs pose every technique from frame data, and it all runs deterministically headless. That made it possible to test matches, the story, and four networked copies in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - the side view's scale is 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2;\n   - props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen;\n   - gamepad codes are released every tick when no pad is connected;\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`, which menu hover relies on;\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key;\n   - the music scheduler's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when the output latency is larger than that.\n3. **The most important thing I built:** the point-sparring core, because the engine's fight module is built around life bars.\n   - `KC.Karateka` covers frame data, height rules, blocking, quality from timing and spacing, counters, and simultaneous hits.\n   - `KC.Bout` runs the referee and judges.\n   - After that, the voice synth and the host-authoritative netcode.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation. Spins turn the rig's facing angle; back kicks strike behind the rig; the bow, seiza, blocks and referee signals come from wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own inverse kinematics.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1: right-stick input codes, so the technique stick can be bound and labelled.\n   - EC2: a game can override a standard setting's default (here, PlayStation glyphs).\n   - EC3: added ✕ ○ □ △ to the font.\n   - EC4: `game.live`, so the world keeps running under the pause menu in online sessions.\n   - EC5: the music look-ahead now covers the output latency.\n   - I also reworded one comment.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid — named poses and joint targets with blend weights, an exported IK helper, and a back-thrust kick. Also a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and during hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar: a named single file, no stubs, live dev sliders, an attract mode, a controller-first 10-foot UI. The rule of one test command that prints only failures kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md uses its own file names (max effort, 17:47:43) while TASK.md wants xhigh and 18:36:38. TASK.md wins, but that's easy to miss.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and token numbers the session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" can be read as either what was allowed or what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the scope in one sitting, all of it tested: 9 matches, 11 locations, 3 bonus games, the lessons, the story, local and online Kachinuki, attract, dev mode, and voices. On top of that, making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I'd change:**\n   - mark which numbers in the plan are tunable targets and which are rules;\n   - give a priority order for when time runs short;\n   - name the target screen (a 1080p TV) and the shape of the hub's frames;\n   - drop the usage line, or say where its numbers should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3480,"cache_read_input_tokens":804006,"cache