{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCFdpUzJVNXSAuUdUkB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nothing has changed, and I'm not starting another pass. The one remaining objection asks for checks only you can supply: playing the game, listening to it, using a real controller, or an outside audit. TASK.md rules out using subagents to audit or judge, and repeating my own automated checks wouldn't add independent evidence.\n\nWhere it stands:\n- The single bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root; the JSON parses and every field is filled.\n- No TODO, FIXME or placeholder text remains in `src/` or the bundle.\n- `node tests/run.mjs` passed 601 checks with 0 failed. Most ran against the shipped file in Chromium.\n\nTo verify it independently, open the `.html` and play the Champon Road with a controller and sound on. If you're satisfied, `/goal clear` ends the loop. If anything is wrong, tell me what and I'll fix it.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional 2.5D \"Tatami\" camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: EC1–EC5), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney and KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I added in game code (bow, seiza, kamae, blocks), spins, somersaults, the referee's signals, the judges' flags and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (pentatonic), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki: winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools, and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count. Screenshots only for what a script can't judge. No subagents or workflows.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs` — 18 suites, 601 checks, including the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's page check, the real AI Hub harness ×4, a browser suite for sound and controller input, and a browser audit of TASK.md.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 2 h 01 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:37:26 by the system clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 22: 13 from the headless suites, 8 from screenshots, and 1 from the browser audit. The audit found the music going silent when the audio output has high latency.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once lunges carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road, plus an honest no-cheat run. It found the soft-lock. Measuring the real audio output was a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It switches through built-in views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, and attack specs pose every technique from frame data. Everything runs deterministically headless, so I could test matches, the story and four networked copies in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - the side view draws 1.5 pixels per world unit, so I made my own views at 1.2;\n   - props placed by world coordinates landed off screen;\n   - gamepad codes are released every tick when no pad is connected;\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`;\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key;\n   - the music's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when the output latency is larger than that.\n3. **The most important thing I built:** the point-sparring core. `KC.Karateka` covers frame data, height rules, blocking, quality, counters and simultaneous hits. `KC.Bout` covers the referee and judges. After those, the voice synth and the netcode, where the host is authoritative.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig, through spins, back kicks, and poses done by wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own IK. All of that while keeping hit detection independent of the animation.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1: right-stick codes, so the technique stick can be bound and labelled.\n   - EC2: overriding a standard setting's default, for PlayStation glyphs.\n   - EC3: ✕ ○ □ △ in the font.\n   - EC4: `game.live`, so a session keeps stepping under the pause menu.\n   - EC5: the music look-ahead now covers the output latency.\n   - Also one reworded comment.\n6. **If I could change one thing:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid (named poses, joint targets with blend weights, an exported IK helper). Also a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the bar. The one-command, failures-only testing rule kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md uses its own file names (max effort, 17:47:43) while TASK.md wants xhigh and 18:36:38;\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and tokens the session can't see;\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the whole scope in one sitting, all of it tested, plus making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I would change:**\n   - mark which plan numbers are tunable targets and which are rules;\n   - give a priority order for when time runs short;\n   - name the target screen and the shape of the hub's frames;\n   - drop the usage line, or say where its numbers come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3388,"cache_read_input_tokens":810807,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3388},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"a4f7ab0f-4772-4604-984c-21008b3df9f1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:41:33.493Z","request_id":"req_011CghCFdRAyccKb4LUbs8uu"}
