{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghCHM6TLrgmso6Ac1qi4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every feature in TASK.md is implemented in the shipped game, with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. Nothing has changed since my last report.\n\nTwo of the checks were written by others, not by me:\n- **AI Hub harness:** the hub's own `harness.html` ran the shipped bundle in all four of the hub's scenarios and reported `window.__hubCheck.pass === true` each time. Those scenarios are 2 players; 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss; the host leaving; and a server copy. It checked the card, ready handling, start, traffic in both directions, a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up and a valid result.\n- **Engine page check:** the engine's own `tools/check.mjs` loaded the bundle over `file://` and found no errors and no external requests. Its screenshots show the scenes drawn.\n\nMy own suites, all run by `node tests/run.mjs`, passed 601 checks with 0 failures. They include a requirement-by-requirement audit of TASK.md run against the shipped file in Chromium:\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Checked by |\n|---|---|\n| The whole game is playable | A full playthrough of the Champon Road through every screen, played and drawn in the browser |\n| Attract mode | It starts after 10 s of real idle and exits on a real key press |\n| Mouse | Real mouse hover and clicks work in the menus |\n| Multiplayer | Player 2 joins with a real \"2\" key |\n| Touch-only devices | The gate and attract mode run on an emulated touch device |\n| Controller | A DualSense through the browser's Gamepad API drives the menus and techniques and gets PlayStation glyphs, with an Xbox option |\n| Audio | The title music's pitches in the output fall on its pentatonic scale, and voices, calls and hits are audible |\n| Dev mode | It opens from the pause menu, its sliders change values live, and reset works |\n| About screen | It contains every exit-interview answer |\n\nOther suites, also passing:\n- **Bonus games:** all three play correctly.\n- **Online:** a fake hub ran 2–4 copies with latency, loss and players leaving.\n- **Versus and local Kachinuki:** both work.\n- **Smoke test:** every stage was checked in both camera views.\n\nFiles:\n- **Bundle:** `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261103-183638.html` is in this folder's root.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses and every field is filled.\n- **Grep:** no TODO, FIXME, XXX or \"placeholder\" text in `src/` or the bundle.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:36:38\n- **Source:** Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; known in Japan as Karate Dō)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages and a mimic-the-master drill; 2D side view on one screen, with an optional 2.5D \"Tatami\" camera\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, modified: EC1–EC5), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, Web Audio; tests on Node 24 with Playwright 1.63.0\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's HD rigs drawn as PS1-era pixel art at 320×240; Japanese stages drawn in code; props from the engine's asset store (Kenney, KayKit, CC0)\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural Humanoid rig, plus karate poses I built on top of it (bow, seiza, kamae, blocks), spins, somersaults, the referee's signals, the judges' flags, and a bull drawn in code\n- **Audio:** chip music written in code (pentatonic), sound effects written in code, and an Animalese-style gibberish voice synth with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions (Kachinuki: winner stays on, 2–4 players), plus local versus and local Kachinuki\n- **Testing:**\n  - Allowed: headless tools and a scripted full playthrough behind one command that prints only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't judge; no subagents or workflows.\n  - Used: `node tests/run.mjs` — 18 suites, 601 checks. These include the full playthrough, a fake hub, the engine's page check, the real AI Hub harness ×4, a browser suite for sound and controller input, and a browser audit of TASK.md.\n- **Research:** the source facts in PLAN.md §1.1; no extra research\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** tokens and cost unknown (no meter available in this session); session time 2 h 01 min of wall clock (18:36:38 to 20:37:26 by the system clock)\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 22. The headless suites caught 13, screenshots caught 8, and the browser audit caught 1: the music going silent on high-latency audio output.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 3:\n   - the story got stuck at the second lesson;\n   - Lesson II couldn't be passed once lunges carried the hero too close;\n   - player 2 on a shared keyboard got the wrong keys.\n3. **The most helpful test:** the scripted playthrough of the whole Champon Road, plus an honest run with no cheats. It found the soft-lock. Measuring the real audio output came a close second.\n4. **The least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` view cycle. It switches through built-in camera views this game never uses, and it flagged a dark cutscene card as low contrast.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the procedural Humanoid rig. One rig with options gives a whole cast, attack specs pose every technique from frame data, and everything runs deterministically without a browser. That let me test matches, the story and four networked copies in a Node `vm`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - The side view draws at a scale of 1.5, so I made my own views at 1.2.\n   - Props I placed by world coordinates landed off screen.\n   - Gamepad codes are released on every tick when no pad is connected.\n   - `input.feed('move')` doesn't update `mouse.t`.\n   - `settings.reset` takes a group, not a key.\n   - The music's 0.15 s look-ahead drops every note when the output latency is larger than that.\n3. **The most important thing I built:** the point-sparring core. `KC.Karateka` handles frame data, height rules, blocks, hit quality, counters and simultaneous hits; `KC.Bout` runs the referee and judges. After that came the voice synth and the netcode, where the host is authoritative.\n4. **The hardest part:** making karate read on the rig while keeping hit detection independent of the animation. That meant spins, back kicks, and poses built by wrapping `rig.solvePose` with my own joint solving.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - EC1 adds right-stick input codes.\n   - EC2 lets a game override a standard setting's default, to make PlayStation glyphs the default.\n   - EC3 adds ✕ ○ □ △ to the font.\n   - EC4 adds `game.live`, so an online session keeps stepping under the pause menu.\n   - EC5 extends the music look-ahead to cover the output latency.\n   - I also reworded one comment.\n6. **One thing I would change:** a built-in pose layer for the Humanoid, with named poses, joint targets with blend weights and an exported joint solver. I'd also add a standard way for the game to keep updating under the pause menu and during hit-stop.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverable and the quality bar. The rule of one test command that prints only failures kept the work honest, and the plan made a big scope buildable.\n2. **What was confusing:**\n   - PLAN.md uses its own file names (max effort, 17:47:43), while TASK.md wants xhigh and 18:36:38.\n   - \"Usage\" asks for cost and tokens the session can't see.\n   - \"Testing: parameters allowed\" reads as both what was allowed and what was done.\n3. **The hardest thing to do:** the full scope in one sitting with all of it tested, plus making two-stick karate read on a procedural rig.\n4. **What I would change:**\n   - mark which numbers in the plan are tunable and which are rules;\n   - give a priority order for when time runs short;\n   - name the target screen and the shape of the hub's frames;\n   - drop the usage line, or say where its numbers should come from."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2849,"cache_read_input_tokens":814195,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2849},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb1a01c-ab9c-4627-8921-fbc800f925bb","uuid":"00320058-0384-4421-a184-51d2b4d46a87","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:42:06.805Z","request_id":"req_011CghCHLZDDjB75QDTmoYVS"}
